		{"id":"bc3d3ee048c08930","type":"file","file":"New Readers Start Here/I - Pleroma Archive/World/Locations/Eos Multiverse/The Dark Forest Zone/Local Cluster/Totum/Verse Structure/Superclusters/Laniakea/Milky Way Galaxy/Sol System/Earth (Terra)/1890-2078/Pre-WW3/Faction Profile/CRINK/The Georgetown Six/The Georgetown Six.md","x":-560,"y":13480,"width":400,"height":400,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"4197c17bc82db1de","type":"text","text":"As part of the long term plan to destabilize any and all non communist societies, the use of illicit substances for various regions serve as a starting point. End result will be the creation and use of an international criminal underworld to be used as the tool for the projects, and to serve as a scapegoat for any future compromises. ","x":630,"y":13518,"width":250,"height":325},
		{"id":"1d70336d64fdfe9e","type":"file","file":"New Readers Start Here/II - Companion Narratives/Syl'Cothan Dominion/1 - Earth/3 - Shadows of Collapse/3 - Great Reset.md","x":109,"y":13880,"width":400,"height":400,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"2ce27dfa3e32882e","type":"text","text":"Yes, during the Stalin era, the use of gulags, concentration camps, and labor camps was widespread in Russia. These camps were used to punish dissenters, prisoners of war, and other undesirables, and were often located in remote and harsh environments such as Siberia or other regions that were difficult to access. These camps were notoriously brutal, with high mortality rates due to poor living conditions, starvation, and forced labor. Inmates were subjected to physical abuse, torture, and political indoctrination, and were often worked to death. The Stalin regime used these camps as a tool of terror to suppress opposition and maintain control over society. The conditions in these camps were intentionally designed to break the will of prisoners and prevent them from ever rebelling against the regime again. In some ways, these camps were not dissimilar to those used by the Nazis during World War II or by the United States government during its internal security efforts. They all shared the common goal of creating a system of punishment that was designed to instill fear and maintain control over society.","x":630,"y":13880,"width":400,"height":629},
		{"id":"aeb61605a1340613","type":"text","text":" As part of “Restoral” prisoners in the gulags that possessed particular skills needed for the project were given fair compensation and insurances to return to the Union with the goal of loyalty and dignity to help retain and contain the disappearances","x":1120,"y":13880,"width":250,"height":285},
		{"id":"a0d614f0885ab360","type":"file","file":"New Readers Start Here/I - Pleroma Archive/World/Historical Eras/I - Lithic Era. (Origins)/(1949) NATO.md","x":109,"y":14280,"width":400,"height":400,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"99a86106efec7db4","type":"file","file":"New Readers Start Here/I - Pleroma Archive/World/Historical Eras/I - Lithic Era. (Origins)/(1950) Korean War.md","x":109,"y":14680,"width":400,"height":400,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"59b2220a5ded28a4","type":"text","text":"Hippy Movement\nThe aftermath of the world wars and the beginning of the cold war induced new means of spreading ideas through a sustainable generation. With some careful influence the new movement became a platform for recreational drug use, sexual experimentation and nonconformist ideals.\n","x":580,"y":14711,"width":250,"height":339},
