		{"id":"9795af73b6ac8897","type":"file","file":"New Readers Start Here/I - Pleroma Archive/World/Historical Eras/I - Lithic Era. (Origins)/(1965) Rise of Socialism.md","x":109,"y":18280,"width":400,"height":400,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"cf1cfcf3f363ce70","type":"text","text":"War on Drugs\nThe war on drugs is a global effort to combat the production, distribution, and consumption of illegal drugs. The war on drugs has been ongoing since the 1970s, with a variety of strategies being implemented by governments to reduce the supply and demand for illicit drugs. One of the main strategies of the war on drugs has been to criminalize drug use and possession, with laws being introduced in many countries that make possession of certain drugs punishable by law. These laws have led to the incarceration of millions of drug users and have contributed to the overcrowding of prisons. The war on drugs has also involved a heavy focus on law enforcement, with governments investing heavily in police and military forces to disrupt drug trafficking networks. This has led to increased surveillance and intelligence-gathering efforts, as well as a heightened militarization of drug control policies. Despite these efforts, the war on drugs has not been entirely successful in reducing drug use or drug-related crime. Many critics argue that the war on drugs has had a disproportionate impact on marginalized communities, while failing to address the underlying social and economic factors that contribute to drug use. Overall, the war on drugs remains a contentious issue, with many advocating for a more nuanced approach that prioritizes harm reduction and public health over strict law enforcement measures.\n","x":600,"y":18224,"width":598,"height":513},
		{"id":"819f1032dc4612a7","type":"text","text":"Drug Smuggling\nBefore the war on drugs, the drug trade was a global industry that involved the production, distribution, and consumption of various illegal substances. These substances included opium, cocaine, heroin, and marijuana, among others. The drug trade was a highly profitable and lucrative industry, with organized crime syndicates and cartels operating in various parts of the world. The drugs were smuggled across borders and sold on the black market, often with little regulation or oversight from governments. The drug trade was associated with violence and social disruption, as it led to the emergence of drug-related gangs and the spread of addiction and health problems. The illicit nature of the trade also contributed to the growth of organized crime and corruption within government agencies. As the demand for drugs increased in the 1960s and 1970s, governments around the world began to crack down on the drug trade, leading to the implementation of the war on drugs. The war on drugs aimed to eradicate the production and distribution of illegal drugs, but it has been criticized for its limited success and its impact on drug users and their rights.\n","x":600,"y":17413,"width":507,"height":534},
		{"id":"bd0354a5c5067e68","type":"file","file":"New Readers Start Here/I - Pleroma Archive/World/Locations/Eos Multiverse/The Dark Forest Zone/Local Cluster/Totum/Verse Structure/Superclusters/Laniakea/Milky Way Galaxy/Sol System/Earth (Terra)/1890-2078/Pre-WW3/Faction Profile/CRINK/The Georgetown Six/Barndyk Foma Sergeyevich.md","x":-760,"y":13995,"width":400,"height":400,"color":"3"},
