		{"id":"705acf7d18c93bbb","type":"file","file":"New Readers Start Here/I - Pleroma Archive/World/Historical Eras/I - Lithic Era. (Origins)/(1962) Cuban Missile Crisis.md","x":109,"y":18681,"width":400,"height":400,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"e92c41b621c1b67c","type":"text","text":"Socialized Education\nThis change in the education system, with a focus on Leninism-Trotskyism, creates a breeding ground for young people who idolize the violent tendencies of communist regimes. By prioritizing violent ideologies, such as Stalinism, and minimizing the negative impact of communism, the youth is encouraged to adopt radical beliefs and behavior.\nThis trend in youth violence can be attributed to the curriculum that fails to emphasize the true nature of communism, which is characterized by mass killings, oppression, and suppression of civil liberties. Instead, the curriculum glorifies the failed policies and ideologies of past communist regimes.\nIt is essential to address this issue in education, by providing a comprehensive and accurate account of the history of communism, including its violent tendencies and the harm it caused to millions of people worldwide. By educating the youth on these issues, we can prevent the perpetuation of violent ideologies and ensure a more peaceful and prosperous society.\n","x":600,"y":18800,"width":513,"height":491},
		{"id":"ae191e5fbb15bf48","type":"file","file":"New Readers Start Here/I - Pleroma Archive/World/Historical Eras/I - Lithic Era. (Origins)/(1963) Assassination.md","x":109,"y":19081,"width":400,"height":400,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"6a497a3022403b5b","type":"text","text":"A Socialist Generation\nThe infiltration of the education system is a critical component of socialism's goal to create a new generation of radicalized socialists. By targeting students at a young age and indoctrinating them with socialist ideologies, the government aims to shape the minds of the next generation.\nThe pre-university stage is crucial as it sets the foundation for the future radicalization of students. By eroding traditional values and replacing them with socialist ideologies, the government aims to create a society that is completely dependent on the state.\nThe final stage of education is where the radicalization truly begins. Students are exposed to even more extreme forms of socialist propaganda and are encouraged to reject traditional beliefs and values. This process ultimately results in the creation of a new generation that is completely subservient to the state and ready to follow socialist policies.\nIt is essential to recognize the dangers of this infiltration and take steps to protect our youth from radical indoctrination. We must prioritize traditional values and education that promotes critical thinking and independent thinking over blind adherence to political ideologies.\n","x":606,"y":19320,"width":592,"height":466},
		{"id":"59aa0e3aab85ec96","type":"file","file":"New Readers Start Here/I - Pleroma Archive/World/Historical Eras/I - Lithic Era. (Origins)/(1969) Moon Landing.md","x":109,"y":19481,"width":400,"height":400,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"53b5bb981aaa3818","type":"text","text":"Falling Behind\nThe Soviet Union launched several failed attempts to land on the moon during the 1960s and 1970s, as part of their ongoing competition with the United States in the space race. The most notable of these failed missions was the Apollo 13 mission, which was launched in 1970 and experienced a catastrophic explosion that nearly killed the astronauts on board. Despite this setback, the Soviet Union continued to push forward with their own moon landing program. One of their most ambitious missions was the Apollo 12 mission, which was launched in 1969 but suffered from technical problems that caused the lunar module to crash upon landing. Another failed mission was the Apollo 14 mission, which successfully landed on the moon in 1971 but faced difficulties during the return journey. The spacecraft's rocket engine malfunctioned, causing it to enter a dangerous orbit around the Earth that nearly resulted in a collision with the atmosphere. The Soviet Union ultimately failed to land a man on the moon, despite several attempts over several years. Their technology was inferior to that of the United States, and they lacked the political will and resources to continue investing in space exploration after their defeat in the space race. Despite these failures, the Soviet Union made significant contributions to space exploration, including the first successful human spaceflight and multiple achievements in rocket technology and spacecraft design.\n","x":606,"y":19840,"width":508,"height":606},
