		{"id":"e3118d12c530e4f4","type":"file","file":"New Readers Start Here/I - Pleroma Archive/World/Historical Eras/I - Lithic Era. (Origins)/(2049) Nitrogen Decline.md","x":109,"y":34283,"width":400,"height":400,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"4cd2e37aeee1ebbd","type":"file","file":"New Readers Start Here/I - Pleroma Archive/World/Locations/Eos Multiverse/The Dark Forest Zone/Local Cluster/Totum/Verse Structure/Superclusters/Laniakea/Milky Way Galaxy/Sol System/Earth (Terra)/1890-2078/Pre-WW3/Faction Profile/Europolis.md","x":109,"y":34683,"width":400,"height":400,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"d7516e87cd6906d9","type":"text","text":"Mass Immigration\nAs a result of the political and economic turmoil that followed the collapse of the American economy, there was a significant influx of migrants leaving the country. These migrants were driven by a variety of factors, including economic hardship, political persecution, and a desire to escape instability and conflict. One of the primary destinations for American migrants was Mexico, as it was located relatively close to the border and offered a relatively stable and welcoming environment. Many migrants made the dangerous journey on foot or by other means, risking their lives to reach safety. Once in Mexico, many migrants faced a variety of challenges, including limited access to resources and jobs, discrimination and xenophobia from local communities, and inadequate healthcare and education services. Some migrants were also targeted by criminal organizations that exploited their vulnerability and desperation for personal gain. In response to this crisis, Mexico implemented a range of policies aimed at providing support and assistance to migrants, including providing temporary shelter, medical care, and job opportunities. The Mexican government also worked with international organizations to develop a coordinated response to the influx of migrants. Despite these efforts, however, the impact of the migration crisis on Mexico's economy and society was significant. The influx of migrants strained resources and put pressure on social services, leading to tension and conflict within communities. Overall, the migration crisis highlighted the challenges that countries face in managing sudden influxes of refugees and displaced populations, and underscored the importance of international cooperation and support in addressing these issues.\n","x":-360,"y":33960,"width":400,"height":200},
		{"id":"577b85e1027a0269","type":"text","text":"European Migrants\nIn the same scenario as American migrants, European migrants also faced significant challenges and difficulties as a result of the collapse of the American economy. Many of these migrants were drawn to Europe by the promise of economic opportunities and political stability, as well as the desire to escape political and social upheaval in their home countries. One of the primary destinations for European migrants was the European Union, which was seen as a bastion of stability and prosperity. However, the EU faced its own challenges in responding to the influx of migrants, including political divisions and resistance from some member states. Many European migrants faced similar challenges to those faced by American migrants, including limited access to resources and jobs, discrimination and xenophobia, and inadequate healthcare and education services. Some migrants also became caught up in criminal networks or exploited by unscrupulous employers. In response to this crisis, the EU implemented a range of policies aimed at providing support and assistance to migrants, including setting up temporary reception centers and developing programs to integrate migrants into local communities. The EU also worked with international organizations to develop a coordinated response to the influx of migrants. However, the response of the EU was not without controversy. Some critics argued that the EU was not doing enough to address the root causes of migration, such as poverty and conflict in migrants' home countries, and that its policies were not sufficient to provide meaningful opportunities for integration and long-term solutions. Overall, the migration crisis in Europe highlighted the challenges that arise when large numbers of people are forced to flee their homes due to economic or political upheaval, and the importance of working together to provide support and assistance to those who are most vulnerable.\n","x":-440,"y":34240,"width":424,"height":336},
		{"id":"a9e4fbb2b7354c43","type":"text","text":"Proxy War in Europe\nProxy wars in Europe have become a common phenomenon in recent years, as the region continues to grapple with the challenges of migration, economic inequality, and political instability. These proxy wars involve the use of intermediaries or surrogates to engage in conflicts on behalf of other parties, often with the aim of destabilizing governments or undermining political agendas. One of the most notable proxy wars in Europe has been the conflict in Ukraine, which erupted in 2014 after the overthrow of a pro-Russian government in Kiev. This conflict has been characterized by proxy battles between Russia and NATO member states, as well as by the involvement of various militia groups and separatist movements. In Ukraine, Russia has been accused of using proxies such as the separatist groups in eastern Ukraine to destabilize the government and undermine its ties to NATO. These proxies have been accused of committing atrocities and targeting civilians, including journalists and aid workers. NATO member states, in turn, have been accused of using their influence to destabilize Russia and weaken its position in the region. This has been particularly evident in the sanctions imposed on Russia after its annexation of Crimea in 2014, as well as in the supply of military aid and training to the Ukrainian government. Other proxy wars in Europe have included the ongoing conflict in Syria, where various regional powers have used proxies such as Islamic extremist groups to further their own interests. This has led to a cycle of violence and instability that has spilled over into neighboring countries such as Turkey, Jordan, and Lebanon. Overall, proxy wars in Europe highlight the dangers of foreign interference in domestic affairs and the importance of maintaining peaceful relations between states. They also underscore the need for transparency and accountability in international relations, as well as for a robust system of checks and balances that can prevent power from being used to undermine democracy and stability.\n","x":-389,"y":34640,"width":373,"height":380},
		{"id":"d1f94e58e3497f71","type":"text","text":"Collusion\nThe globalists, despite their control over governments and political institutions, face a constant and persistent challenge from third parties. These third parties are organizations, groups, or individuals who operate outside of traditional political structures and use various tactics to influence elections and media coverage. These groups often target specific issues or populations that are ignored or marginalized by the mainstream media and political parties. They may focus on social justice, environmentalism, or economic inequality, among other topics. They are not always politically motivated, but they often act in ways that challenge the status quo and call for change. In this world, the deep state's attempts to manipulate elections and control the media are constantly thwarted by these third parties. Even with their vast resources and connections, the globalists find themselves unable to silence or suppress these groups completely. As a result, every few elections, the globalists find themselves on the losing side. This is a source of constant frustration and anger for them, as they see their carefully crafted strategies and schemes fall apart time and again. They try to counter these groups with even more powerful tactics, such as propaganda, fear-mongering, and misinformation campaigns. However, despite their best efforts, these groups continue to gain ground and influence over time. The more they succeed in mobilizing public opinion and shifting political momentum, the more the globalists feel threatened and cornered. In this world, the ongoing battle between the globalists and third parties is a defining feature of modern politics. It is a struggle for power, influence, and control over society's values and priorities. And while it is often bitter and divisive, it ultimately serves as a reminder that democracy and freedom can never truly be taken for granted.\n","x":-233,"y":33199,"width":273,"height":169},
